            <ArticleTitle>Malignant systemic hypertension, encephalopathy and bradycardia following splenectomy for hereditary spherocytosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2014-209029</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2014209029</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An 8-year-old girl suffering from hereditary spherocytosis underwent splenectomy for chronic severe anaemia. Surgery was uneventful and the patient had a good early postoperative recovery. On the third postoperative day, however, she developed severe headache with associated abnormal movements of upper limbs and nystagmus. She had a heart rate of 50 bpm and a blood pressure of 180/110 mm Hg. She was managed with triple antihypertensives, antiepileptics and sedatives. She recovered slowly over 2 weeks and is fine at 5 months follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2015 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
